course: dictionary
lesson: 2329
title: Crucial and key words
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Crucial» means extremely important. If something is crucial, other things depend on it, so it cannot go without it. «Sleep is crucial for growing children» means children cannot grow well without enough sleep.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues: «crucial to», «crucial for», «crucial role» and «crucial moment» all point to importance. To check your meaning, swap in «very important». «Sleep is very important for growing children» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: In an old-fashioned UK slang sense, «crucial» can mean brilliant. You will hardly ever need that meaning. In a history text, «Sahaidachny» and «hetman» may be new names, but the important word is «crucial»: «the Cossacks were crucial, but the army was a joint one» means the Cossacks were very important to the army.
fact: Crucial means extremely important, because something else depends on it.
fact: «Crucial» is often used in science, maths, sport and safety advice.
fact: Useful phrases with «crucial» are «crucial to», «crucial for», «crucial role», «crucial moment» and «absolutely crucial».
fact: «It is crucial to wear a helmet» means that wearing a helmet matters a great deal for your safety.
fact: The old UK slang meaning, brilliant or excellent, is out of date, so do not use it in school writing.
exercise: «Sleep is crucial for growing children.» What does crucial mean? | Quiet and calm | Very tired | *Extremely important | Hard to see
exercise: In sport reports, «a crucial goal» decides the match. What does crucial tell you about the goal? | It was the goal that counted least | *It mattered most to the result | It was scored with the left foot | It was scored by a goalkeeper
exercise: «It is crucial to wear a helmet.» Which word could replace crucial without changing the meaning? | Very funny | *Very important | Very cold | Very small
test: «Finding the right key was crucial to opening the door.» What does crucial mean here? | *Very important, because the door could not open without it | Very noisy | Very old | Very wet
test: Fill the gap: The last kick was the ___ moment of the match. | soft | *crucial | cheap | sleepy
exam: In this sentence, «the Cossacks were crucial, but the army was a joint one», what does crucial mean? | Very old and worn | *Extremely important to the army | Loud and noisy | Lost in the battle
